ALVA — Standardize Touch-Control Icons and Personalization Screen

Objective

Update Alva’s mobile touch-control interface to make the universal action icons clearer and ensure the button personalization screen always displays neutral, character-independent icons.

Repository: hiyroscript/alva
Branch: main

This is a UI presentation update, not a gameplay or input-system redesign. Preserve all combat mechanics, button bindings, control positions, customization functionality, and character-specific gameplay abilities.

1. Jump button — Remove the bottom line

Primary file: js/ui/icons.js

The current ICONS.jump SVG displays an upward-pointing arrow with a separate horizontal line beneath it.

Change the icon so that:

* Only the upward-pointing arrow remains.
* Remove the separate horizontal baseline below the arrow.
* Preserve the arrow’s vertical shaft and arrowhead.
* Keep the icon centered and visually balanced.
* Preserve the existing stroke thickness, SVG dimensions, and color inheritance.

The current SVG includes:

<path d="M5 20.5h14"/>

Remove that specific bottom line without removing the actual arrow shaft.

Apply the updated Jump icon everywhere it is used, including battle controls, Practice Ground, and the touch-control customization screen.

Do not change the Jump action, jump physics, multiple-jump behavior, or input binding.

2. Defence button — Always display the shield icon

Primary files:

* js/ui/touch-controls.js
* js/ui/icons.js

Currently, the defence button changes its displayed icon depending on whether the fighter is grounded or airborne.

On the ground, it shows ICONS.shield.

In the air, when a fighter has Deflect, it switches to ICONS.deflect.

Remove this visual icon-switching behavior.

Required behavior:

* The defence button must always display ICONS.shield.
* This applies on the ground and in the air.
* It applies to every playable character.
* It applies in regular battles, Practice Ground, and the touch-control editor.
* The shield icon must not change when the fighter jumps, falls, lands, or enters another combat state.

In TouchControls.refreshAbilities(), stop replacing the shield’s artwork with ICONS.deflect.

Important: Preserve the actual Shield and Deflect mechanics. An airborne press must still perform Deflect when supported by the fighter. The button must continue sending the existing shield input.

The accessibility label may continue identifying the airborne action as “Deflect” when appropriate, because the actual action changes. Only the visible icon must remain the shield.

Do not change the defensive mechanics, Energy consumption, timing windows, or combat behavior.

3. Mouvement buttons — Use double arrows

Primary files:

* js/ui/icons.js
* js/ui/touch-controls.js

In the Joystick control scheme, there are two separate Mouvement buttons:

* mouvementLeft
* mouvementRight

These currently display single directional arrow icons.

Replace them with double directional arrows.

Desired appearance

* Left Mouvement: two arrows pointing left, visually similar to ≪.
* Right Mouvement: two arrows pointing right, visually similar to ≫.
* Each button should contain two distinct arrowheads pointing in the same direction.
* Use clean SVG geometry consistent with the current icon system.
* Keep the double arrows centered inside their circular buttons.
* Maintain adequate spacing and legibility at small mobile sizes.

Prefer adding dedicated universal icons to ICONS, such as mouvementLeft and mouvementRight, or similarly descriptive names.

Update MOUVEMENT_BUTTONS to reference the new icons.

The Classic Buttons scheme’s ordinary runLeft and runRight controls should retain their existing single-arrow appearance. The double-arrow design is specifically for the Joystick scheme’s Mouvement/Dash buttons.

Preserve the existing internal codenames, spelling, input events, Dash/air-dash logic, and touch behavior.

4. Button personalization screen — Always show default icons

Primary files:

* js/ui/touch-layout-editor.js
* js/ui/touch-controls.js
* js/ui/mobile-abilities.js

This is an important distinction between the customization editor and actual gameplay.

Currently, the touch-control editor displays character-specific attack artwork based on the fighter last selected.

This is not the desired behavior.

The button personalization screen must always show neutral default icons, completely independent of the selected character.

Default icon mapping

Control	Required icon
Attack 1	One dot (ICONS.pip1)
Attack 2	Two dots (ICONS.pip2)
Attack 3	Three dots (ICONS.pip3)
Attack 4	Four dots (ICONS.pip4)
Attack 5	Five dots (ICONS.pip5)
Extra Attack	Universal ring (ICONS.ring)
Transform	Universal star (ICONS.transform)
Defence	Universal shield (ICONS.shield)
Jump	Updated upward arrow without baseline
Left Mouvement	Double left arrows
Right Mouvement	Double right arrows
Classic Left / Right	Existing single left/right arrows
Joystick	Existing universal joystick appearance

The neutral attack pips, ring, Transform star, shield, and other universal SVGs already exist. Reuse them rather than creating duplicate assets.

Required editor behavior

1. Opening Settings → Controls → Customize touch controls must display the default icons for every button.
2. No attack button may display a fighter sprite, ability preview image, animation frame, projectile image, or character-specific icon.
3. Every attack slot from Attack 1 through Attack 5 must remain visible, even if the currently selected character does not possess that attack.
4. The icons must not depend on the player’s last selected fighter.
5. Switching characters elsewhere must never change the editor’s icons.
6. Reopening the editor must produce the same neutral icon mapping.
7. Switching between Joystick and Classic layouts must preserve the appropriate neutral icons.
8. The editor should use neutral, localized control names such as “Attack 1”, “Attack 2”, and “Extra Attack”, rather than character-specific move names.
9. Existing resizing, dragging, control selection, keyboard/gamepad editing, layout persistence, and Reset to Defaults must work exactly as before.

Implementation guidance

In TouchLayoutEditor.open(), the current implementation calls:

this.touch.setCharacter(getPlayableCharacter(this.app.selection?.characterId));

This makes the editor’s button presentation depend on the selected character.

Use the neutral rendering path instead, such as this.touch.setCharacter(null), while preserving the editor’s showAbsent: true behavior.

Check that the neutral path consistently produces default icons and labels for every control.

Avoid unnecessarily changing the rendering behavior of the actual battle interface.

Important separation

In the personalization screen: Always show neutral icons.

In real gameplay: Continue displaying each fighter’s authored attack artwork wherever that is the existing intended behavior.

The universal changes to Jump, Defence, and Mouvement icons should apply in both contexts.

Do not remove or modify the character artwork assets, character definitions, move previews, or abilities simply to achieve the neutral editor appearance.

5. Preserve the existing control system

The implementation must maintain all existing interaction behavior.

In particular:

* Do not modify the input codenames shield, jump, attack1 through attack5, extra_attack, transform, mouvementLeft, or mouvementRight.
* Preserve the existing data-action, data-mouvement, and data-control identifiers.
* Preserve multi-touch support, simultaneous button presses, pointer capture, and pressed states.
* Preserve the existing button positions, sizing, hit targets, and honeycomb attack layout.
* Do not reset or migrate the user’s saved custom layouts unnecessarily.
* Preserve localization in English and French.
* Do not modify game physics, character data, damage, cooldowns, or movement mechanics.
* Do not modify the protected character_rule file.

Keep the solution simple, reusable, and compatible with additional characters introduced in the future.

